{"evidence":[{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Skyscraper is an upcoming roller coaster that will be located at the planned Skyplex complex in Orlando, Florida.","title":"Skyscraper (roller coaster)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Under development by American and Swiss manufacturers US Thrill Rides and Intamin, the attraction will be a Polercoaster model which utilizes an observation tower as its main support structure.","title":"Skyscraper (roller coaster)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" After several delays, Skyscraper is expected to open in 2020 with construction on the Skyplex complex beginning in 2017.","title":"Skyscraper (roller coaster)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Upon completion, it will become the tallest roller coaster in the world at over 500 ft and will feature both the steepest drop and highest inversion.","title":"Skyscraper (roller coaster)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Millennium Force is a steel roller coaster built by Intamin at Cedar Point amusement park in Sandusky, Ohio, United States.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" It was the fourteenth roller coaster to be built at the park since Blue Streak opened in 1964.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Upon completion in 2000, Millennium Force broke six world records and was the world's first Giga Coaster, a roller coaster that exceeds 300 ft in height and completes a full circuit.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" It was briefly the tallest and fastest in the world until Steel Dragon 2000 opened later the same year.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The ride is also the third-longest roller coaster in North America after The Beast at Kings Island and Fury 325 at Carowinds.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It was the first roller coaster to use a cable lift system rather than a traditional chain lift.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":6,"text":" The coaster has a 310 ft , 45-degree lift hill with a 300 ft drop and features two tunnels, three overbanked turns, and four hills.","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":7,"text":" It has a top speed of 93 mph .","title":"Millennium Force"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Magnum XL-200, colloquially known as simply Magnum, is a steel roller coaster built by Arrow Dynamics at Cedar Point in Sandusky, Ohio.","title":"Magnum XL-200"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" When built in 1989, it was the tallest, fastest, and steepest complete-circuit roller coaster in the world as well as the first hypercoaster – a roller coaster that exceeds 200 ft in height.","title":"Magnum XL-200"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Some have credited Magnum with starting a period in the industry known as the "roller coaster wars", in which amusement parks competed with one another at a rapid pace to build the next tallest and fastest roller coaster.","title":"Magnum XL-200"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" More than 40 million people had ridden Magnum as of 2009.","title":"Magnum XL-200"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"The Cyclone was a wooden roller coaster that operated at Revere Beach in Revere, Massachusetts, from 1925 until 1969.","title":"Cyclone (Revere Beach)"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" When Cyclone was constructed, it was the tallest roller coaster ever built, as well as being the first roller coaster in the world to reach 100 ft in height.","title":"Cyclone (Revere Beach)"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" In addition to being the tallest roller coaster of its day, some also claim that it was the largest and fastest roller coaster in the world, with a length of 3600 ft and top speeds between 45 and 50 mph (some dispute the speed record claim and instead award that honor to the Giant Dipper).","title":"Cyclone (Revere Beach)"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" Cyclone held the title of world's tallest roller coaster until 1964 when it was surpassed by Montaña Rusa at La Feria Chapultepec Mágico in Mexico City, Mexico.","title":"Cyclone (Revere Beach)"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Moonsault Scramble was a shuttle roller coaster that operated from 1983 until 2000 at Fuji-Q Highland amusement park in Fujiyoshida, Yamanashi, Japan.","title":"Moonsault Scramble"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" According to the "Guinness Book of Records", Moonsault Scramble was the tallest roller coaster in the world until 1996, when its record height was surpassed by another roller coaster at Fuji-Q Highland called Fujiyama, which reached 79 m in height.","title":"Moonsault Scramble"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Moonsault Scramble was the first roller coaster to surpass 61 metres (200 ft) in height, and it remains the third tallest shuttle roller coaster ever built, behind (Six Flags Magic Mountain), and Tower of Terror II (Dreamworld) The coaster was removed from the park to make way for the construction of Dodonpa in 2001.","title":"Moonsault Scramble"},{"sentence_id":0,"text":"Tatsu is a steel flying roller coaster designed by Bolliger & Mabillard at the Six Flags Magic Mountain amusement park located in Valencia, California, United States.","title":"Tatsu"},{"sentence_id":1,"text":" Announced on November 17, 2005, the roller coaster opened to the public on May 13, 2006 as the park's seventeenth roller coaster.","title":"Tatsu"},{"sentence_id":2,"text":" Tatsu reaches a height of 170 ft and speeds up to 62 mph .","title":"Tatsu"},{"sentence_id":3,"text":" The ride's name means "Flying Beast" in Japanese.","title":"Tatsu"},{"sentence_id":4,"text":" The roller coaster is also the world's tallest and fastest flying coaster; is the only flying roller coaster to feature a zero-gravity roll; and has the world's highest pretzel loop.","title":"Tatsu"},{"sentence_id":5,"text":" It was the world's longest flying coaster until The Flying Dinosaur surpassed it.","title":"Tatsu"}],"peer_messages":["Unverified peer answer: Skyscraper
